  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure will remain in control through \ntonight, bringing cooler and less humid conditions. \n\nHigh pressure will remain in place through tonight, with a \ncooler and much drier airmass in place. After a nice day with\ntemperatures only in the mid 70s to around 80, a cool night is\nin store with lows mainly in the mid 50s to low 60s. Some patchy\nfog cannot be ruled out in sheltered areas as winds will be \nvery light to calm overnight allowing temperatures to radiate\ndown to near-saturation, especially in areas that have received\nsubstantial rainfall recently and have residual ground moisture.\nIncreasing clouds late should temper this potential enough that\nwidespread or dense fog appears unlikely. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warmer and more humid conditions return Sunday,\nalong with daily chances for showers and thunderstorms into the\nmiddle of the week.\n\nBroad upper level ridging returns to the eastern US early next \nweek, bringing in surface high pressure across the eastern US. \nAfternoon highs will creep back into the mid 80s to low 90s, \nabout 5 degrees above average for early September. Dewpoints \nwill also creep back into the upper 60s to low 70s.\n\nConvection potential will return starting Sunday, although any\nshowers and storms should remain fairly isolated to widely\nscattered in nature and focused mainly along and NW of the I-95\ncorridor. Instability will be the limiting factor. Severe \nstorms are unlikely, though gusty winds, small hail, and brief \nheavy downpours are possible with any stronger storms.\n\nOne other aspect to watch with this ridging pattern is the \npotential for thunderstorms to rotate around the periphery of \nthe ridge and the position of a warm front next week. The exact \nlocation, timing, and intensity of the thunderstorms remains a \nbit uncertain at this range. Currently, the main days to monitor\nare Tuesday and Wednesday regarding heavy rain and severe \nweather potential.",
